WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.186 --> 00:00:02.636
- Hi everyone, Sal Khan here.

00:00:02.636 --> 00:00:05.566
Welcome to the Homeroom
with Sal livestream.

00:00:05.566 --> 00:00:08.456
We have a very, very
exciting conversation today

00:00:08.456 --> 00:00:09.716
with Jennifer Doudna,

00:00:09.716 --> 00:00:13.136
the 2020 Nobel prize winner in chemistry.

00:00:13.136 --> 00:00:15.116
For what has often been described as

00:00:15.116 --> 00:00:18.046
one of the most important discoveries

00:00:18.046 --> 00:00:20.006
in the history of biology

00:00:20.006 --> 00:00:21.776
or maybe in the history of science.

00:00:21.776 --> 00:00:23.476
And we'll talk about why that is.

00:00:23.476 --> 00:00:25.446
It could actually forever change

00:00:25.446 --> 00:00:27.126
even what it means to be human.

00:00:27.126 --> 00:00:29.176
But before we go into that,

00:00:29.176 --> 00:00:31.226
I will give my standard announcements

00:00:31.226 --> 00:00:33.916
a reminder that we are and
not-for-profit Khan Academy.

00:00:33.916 --> 00:00:35.086
So if you're in a position to do so,

00:00:35.086 --> 00:00:38.506
please think about going
to Khan academy.org/donate.

00:00:38.506 --> 00:00:40.565
Donations of all size,
make a huge difference.

00:00:40.565 --> 00:00:44.276
Also wanna give a special shout
out to several organizations

00:00:44.276 --> 00:00:47.436
that stepped up, especially
during COVID when they realized

00:00:47.436 --> 00:00:48.846
more people were depending on us.

00:00:48.846 --> 00:00:51.296
And we were running at a deficit.

00:00:51.296 --> 00:00:53.936
Special thanks to Bank of America, AT&amp;T,

00:00:53.936 --> 00:00:56.486
Google.org, Novartis, Fastly.

00:00:56.486 --> 00:00:58.876
And it was just announced
yesterday General Motors

00:00:58.876 --> 00:01:01.066
for very, very generous support

00:01:01.066 --> 00:01:05.316
to allow us to help millions
of y'all keep learning

00:01:06.436 --> 00:01:08.506
this year and last year.

00:01:08.506 --> 00:01:11.586
Also wanna remind you that
there's a podcast version

00:01:11.586 --> 00:01:14.476
of this live stream, wherever
you get your podcasts

00:01:14.476 --> 00:01:17.056
Homeroom with Sal, the podcast.

00:01:17.056 --> 00:01:21.306
With that, I'm very, very
excited to introduce Jennifer

00:01:21.306 --> 00:01:24.566
who is a, you are the li Ka-shing

00:01:24.566 --> 00:01:27.936
chancellor's chair professor
at the department of chemistry

00:01:27.936 --> 00:01:30.519
and department of
molecular and cell bio...

00:01:30.519 --> 00:01:31.352
You have a very long title.

00:01:31.352 --> 00:01:32.616
Department of cell molecular biology

00:01:32.616 --> 00:01:35.066
at the University of California, Berkeley.

00:01:35.066 --> 00:01:38.766
You got the 2020 Nobel prize in chemistry

00:01:38.766 --> 00:01:40.836
for your work with CRISPR.

00:01:40.836 --> 00:01:42.659
Thank you so much for joining us.

00:01:43.496 --> 00:01:45.846
- Thanks for inviting me
Sal, great to be here.

00:01:48.532 --> 00:01:51.486
- Those of y'all watching
who have not heard of CRISPR

00:01:51.486 --> 00:01:54.560
this will be, I predict a
hundred years in the future.

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:58.383
All of the other craziness
of 2020 will kind of

00:01:58.383 --> 00:02:03.296
not be remembered as much
as the work that Jennifer

00:02:03.296 --> 00:02:04.306
and her colleagues have done

00:02:04.306 --> 00:02:07.266
over the last several years in CRISPR.

00:02:07.266 --> 00:02:11.469
Jennifer, for those of you
who are unfamiliar with it

00:02:11.469 --> 00:02:15.086
what's kind of the headline
or the cliff notes version

00:02:15.086 --> 00:02:17.419
of what CRISPR is and why it's important?

00:02:18.366 --> 00:02:20.056
- Well, I guess I would start by saying

00:02:20.056 --> 00:02:22.826
it's a technology for genome editing.

00:02:22.826 --> 00:02:23.946
What does that mean?

00:02:23.946 --> 00:02:26.806
Well, it's a tool that
scientists are using to make

00:02:26.806 --> 00:02:31.456
precise changes to the DNA
in a cell or a whole organism

00:02:31.456 --> 00:02:34.956
that can lead to alterations
in the genetic makeup

00:02:34.956 --> 00:02:38.641
of the cell or the resulting organisms.

00:02:38.641 --> 00:02:39.956
So we're, ah, we've got the video here.

00:02:39.956 --> 00:02:43.336
We're seeing the CRISPR system in action.

00:02:43.336 --> 00:02:46.886
So it's a protein that purple blob

00:02:46.886 --> 00:02:50.096
with a little zip code molecule in it

00:02:50.096 --> 00:02:53.766
a piece of RNA that allows binding

00:02:53.766 --> 00:02:58.766
to a 20 letter sequence
in the DNA of a cell.

00:02:58.796 --> 00:03:03.486
And so you can see the
guide RNA marking the site

00:03:03.486 --> 00:03:05.326
and then DNA getting cut.

00:03:05.326 --> 00:03:08.316
That's the T activity of CRISPR.

00:03:08.316 --> 00:03:13.316
And what that does is to
induce a DNA repair pathway

00:03:13.506 --> 00:03:18.356
in cells that allows cells
to alter the DNA sequence

00:03:18.356 --> 00:03:20.806
at that precise position.

00:03:20.806 --> 00:03:22.916
And in the example, we
just saw in the video

00:03:22.916 --> 00:03:26.366
there was insertion of a new piece of DNA

00:03:26.366 --> 00:03:28.956
that could allow cells
to acquire a new gene

00:03:28.956 --> 00:03:30.826
or even a new set of genes.

00:03:30.826 --> 00:03:33.886
And this is all now being
controlled by scientists.

00:03:33.886 --> 00:03:37.096
So we can manipulate genes
in ways that were previously

00:03:37.096 --> 00:03:38.969
very difficult or impossible.

00:03:40.526 --> 00:03:43.236
- Yeah, and for those watching
who didn't fully appreciate

00:03:43.236 --> 00:03:46.106
how powerful what some
of what you just said is

00:03:46.106 --> 00:03:48.442
obviously are, I mean,
we're living systems

00:03:48.442 --> 00:03:50.170
everything else is...

00:03:50.170 --> 00:03:53.831
All living systems have DNA.

00:03:53.831 --> 00:03:57.266
You have these long sequences
of DNA, parts of them.

00:03:57.266 --> 00:04:00.206
Those are individual genes
that can code for proteins

00:04:00.206 --> 00:04:01.086
or sets of proteins.

00:04:01.086 --> 00:04:04.036
And now using CRISPR,
y'all have introduced

00:04:04.036 --> 00:04:08.488
a technology that can help us edit DNA.

00:04:08.488 --> 00:04:11.666
Before I go into all of
the implications positive

00:04:11.666 --> 00:04:15.432
and potentially ones that
we have to wonder about,

00:04:15.432 --> 00:04:19.306
I'm curious about how your pathway

00:04:19.306 --> 00:04:21.800
to kind of discovering this.

00:04:21.800 --> 00:04:24.076
When did it first dawn on you

00:04:24.076 --> 00:04:26.869
that something like this could happen?

00:04:28.151 --> 00:04:30.596
And how hard or easy was
it to get to the place

00:04:30.596 --> 00:04:31.429
that you got to?

00:04:32.766 --> 00:04:35.646
- Well, you know Sal,
what I love about CRISPR,

00:04:35.646 --> 00:04:38.666
one of the things is the origin story.

00:04:38.666 --> 00:04:41.216
Because this is really
a technology that came

00:04:41.216 --> 00:04:45.176
out of a fundamental
curiosity driven project.

00:04:45.176 --> 00:04:47.526
It was small science, not big science.

00:04:47.526 --> 00:04:50.856
It was science being done by
a handful of laboratories.

00:04:50.856 --> 00:04:53.976
And in our own lab at University
of California, Berkeley

00:04:53.976 --> 00:04:58.076
we were investigating
CRISPR, which was originally

00:04:58.076 --> 00:05:01.316
discovered to be a
bacterial immune system,

00:05:01.316 --> 00:05:05.836
a way that bacteria can
detect and destroy viruses.

00:05:05.836 --> 00:05:07.846
And when I heard about
this from my colleague,

00:05:07.846 --> 00:05:11.596
Jillian Banfield at Berkeley,
probably a dozen years ago now

00:05:11.596 --> 00:05:12.526
I was fascinated.

00:05:12.526 --> 00:05:14.376
I wondered, well, how does that work?

00:05:14.376 --> 00:05:16.479
And so we began investigating it.

00:05:17.959 --> 00:05:20.416
- People have known about
CRISPR for some time.

00:05:20.416 --> 00:05:21.976
If this stands for

00:05:21.976 --> 00:05:26.136
Clustered Regularly Interspaced
Short Palindromic Repeats.

00:05:26.136 --> 00:05:28.909
But really what that is is these bacteria

00:05:28.909 --> 00:05:32.199
would have these sequences of
what would it be DNA or RNA-

00:05:33.609 --> 00:05:34.442
- DNA.

00:05:34.442 --> 00:05:37.166
- DNA, and it would have pieces of kind of

00:05:37.166 --> 00:05:40.126
ancient other viruses in it.

00:05:40.126 --> 00:05:42.703
And so it would use those as kind of

00:05:42.703 --> 00:05:45.126
that tagging location sequence

00:05:45.126 --> 00:05:47.296
the way that you just described
in that previous visual

00:05:47.296 --> 00:05:50.756
to kind of cut viral DNA,
is that what it was doing?

00:05:50.756 --> 00:05:52.106
- Yes, that's right.

00:05:52.106 --> 00:05:54.586
And by the way not necessarily ancient

00:05:54.586 --> 00:05:56.826
pieces of virus either.

00:05:56.826 --> 00:05:59.896
This is a system that works in real time.

00:05:59.896 --> 00:06:04.017
So during an infection, a
bacterial cell could acquire

00:06:04.017 --> 00:06:08.676
a DNA sequence from a virus
and then use it as the template

00:06:08.676 --> 00:06:12.036
to make a guide RNA molecule that allows

00:06:12.036 --> 00:06:15.176
this kind of searching that
you're seeing going on here

00:06:15.176 --> 00:06:17.249
by the CAS9 protein.

00:06:18.166 --> 00:06:21.949
So this is a system that started

00:06:21.949 --> 00:06:25.491
as a fundamental biology project.

00:06:25.491 --> 00:06:29.567
But once Emmanuel
Charpentier our collaborator

00:06:29.567 --> 00:06:34.056
in our lab figured out
how the CAS9 protein works

00:06:34.056 --> 00:06:38.096
as a DNA cleaver, we could
see how it could be harnessed

00:06:38.096 --> 00:06:40.106
as a genome editing technology.

00:06:40.106 --> 00:06:41.556
And that's really, what's taken off

00:06:41.556 --> 00:06:42.856
over the last eight years.

00:06:44.086 --> 00:06:46.506
- When did you first learn about CRISPR

00:06:46.506 --> 00:06:49.266
and that bacteria were using it

00:06:49.266 --> 00:06:51.936
in order to be your
viral defense mechanism

00:06:51.936 --> 00:06:53.016
and it dawned on you that,

00:06:53.016 --> 00:06:55.316
hey, maybe that could be used to edit DNA?

00:06:55.316 --> 00:06:57.446
When was this and then how
long before you started

00:06:57.446 --> 00:06:59.496
making some significant progress on that?

00:07:00.776 --> 00:07:03.906
- I think I first heard
about CRISPR in the mid 2000s

00:07:03.906 --> 00:07:07.073
from Jillian Banfield, at Berkeley

00:07:07.073 --> 00:07:09.896
who was researching this in bacteria.

00:07:09.896 --> 00:07:13.766
She was looking at DNA
sequences in bacteria.

00:07:13.766 --> 00:07:16.326
And she was one of the first laboratories

00:07:16.326 --> 00:07:21.326
to realize that bacteria were
acquiring DNA and storing it

00:07:22.496 --> 00:07:25.336
in their own genetic material in real time

00:07:25.336 --> 00:07:27.226
in response to viral infection,

00:07:27.226 --> 00:07:29.526
sort of a fascinating observation.

00:07:29.526 --> 00:07:32.316
And at that time it was a hypothesis

00:07:32.316 --> 00:07:34.736
that this might be some
kind of immune system.

00:07:34.736 --> 00:07:36.736
And that's really where we got involved.

00:07:37.891 --> 00:07:42.891
- Initially, were you like,
this is like a 5% chance

00:07:43.416 --> 00:07:45.586
of this actually leading to anything?

00:07:45.586 --> 00:07:48.026
Did people think that you were kind of

00:07:48.026 --> 00:07:50.036
going down on a wild goose chase?

00:07:50.036 --> 00:07:54.219
Or was it apparent pretty early
that no, this is a big idea?

00:07:56.006 --> 00:07:57.776
- Little of both, I guess.

00:07:57.776 --> 00:08:02.106
I certainly felt like it was
a very interesting biological

00:08:02.106 --> 00:08:04.796
phenomenon that I wanted to pursue.

00:08:04.796 --> 00:08:09.356
At the same time, I was a
little bit nervous about

00:08:10.515 --> 00:08:14.766
using biomedical research
funding to work on this

00:08:14.766 --> 00:08:17.686
because it wasn't entirely
clear where it was headed

00:08:17.686 --> 00:08:20.496
in terms of ultimate outcomes.

00:08:20.496 --> 00:08:23.006
And I can remember getting
a few strange looks

00:08:23.006 --> 00:08:25.656
at scientific meetings when I first began

00:08:25.656 --> 00:08:27.396
talking about our work on CRISPR

00:08:27.396 --> 00:08:30.466
because at that time almost
nobody had heard of it.

00:08:30.466 --> 00:08:33.479
And it sounded like a real
niche area of biology.

00:08:34.836 --> 00:08:37.476
- What was the moment
and when was the moment

00:08:37.476 --> 00:08:41.989
where you said, oh my God,
this works, this works?

00:08:44.320 --> 00:08:46.176
One of my favorite papers

00:08:46.176 --> 00:08:47.836
which I'm sure you're
very familiar with is

00:08:47.836 --> 00:08:49.846
Watson and Crick paper, where
they found the structure

00:08:49.846 --> 00:08:51.636
of the double helix structure of DNA.

00:08:51.636 --> 00:08:54.207
Which is a one and a 1/2
page paper at the end

00:08:54.207 --> 00:08:55.336
I think they say something like

00:08:55.336 --> 00:08:56.606
it has not passed our attention,

00:08:56.606 --> 00:08:58.259
the implications of this word.

00:08:59.156 --> 00:09:00.316
When was that moment for you?

00:09:00.316 --> 00:09:02.566
When did it happen and
what did it just feel like?

00:09:02.566 --> 00:09:03.399
And how did you know

00:09:03.399 --> 00:09:05.539
that you'd kind of gotten to something?

00:09:07.016 --> 00:09:09.316
Well, it was somewhat of
a continuum, I would say.

00:09:09.316 --> 00:09:11.066
Towards the end of the 2000s,

00:09:11.066 --> 00:09:14.816
we were working on
various different aspects

00:09:14.816 --> 00:09:16.776
of the CRISPR system and bacteria.

00:09:16.776 --> 00:09:20.426
Mostly doing biochemical
experiments in the lab,

00:09:20.426 --> 00:09:23.056
meaning working with
purified proteins and RNAs

00:09:23.056 --> 00:09:26.256
and DNA molecules to
figure out the chemistry

00:09:26.256 --> 00:09:28.296
of how they work together.

00:09:28.296 --> 00:09:31.386
And then that work really culminated

00:09:31.386 --> 00:09:35.556
in the work that we published in 2012

00:09:35.556 --> 00:09:39.334
where we described the way
that the CAS9 protein works

00:09:39.334 --> 00:09:43.916
as an RNA guided molecular
scissors that can cut DNA

00:09:43.916 --> 00:09:45.446
at a desired place.

00:09:45.446 --> 00:09:48.526
And importantly, that we
as scientists could control

00:09:48.526 --> 00:09:51.356
where the cutting
happens by programming it

00:09:51.356 --> 00:09:52.896
with these molecules of RNA.

00:09:52.896 --> 00:09:54.596
And I think for us that was really

00:09:55.765 --> 00:09:59.076
the publication that, where
we were able to point out

00:09:59.076 --> 00:10:01.879
that this could be used
as a gene editing tool.

00:10:03.526 --> 00:10:05.299
And I just wanna make sure I understand.

00:10:05.299 --> 00:10:07.143
Actually, you can move over a little bit

00:10:07.143 --> 00:10:10.482
I think to the right, your camera is-

00:10:10.482 --> 00:10:11.987
- I'm drifting-

00:10:11.987 --> 00:10:13.154
- Much better.

00:10:16.407 --> 00:10:19.239
Just to make sure I understand
what makes the science

00:10:19.239 --> 00:10:20.686
so it makes sense to me that you have

00:10:20.686 --> 00:10:25.526
this kind of protein enzyme
complex that has a strand of RNA

00:10:25.526 --> 00:10:29.096
on it and that RNA can find
the complimentary part of DNA.

00:10:29.096 --> 00:10:31.176
And then this protein is able to cut there

00:10:31.176 --> 00:10:32.926
and maybe in another place as well,

00:10:32.926 --> 00:10:35.376
maybe based on another RNA strand.

00:10:35.376 --> 00:10:36.406
How do you insert?

00:10:36.406 --> 00:10:38.346
I mean, in that visual we saw

00:10:38.346 --> 00:10:40.016
that there was that step where...

00:10:41.836 --> 00:10:44.916
Does the DNA just kind of float in?

00:10:44.916 --> 00:10:46.946
And if you put the right
enzymes, it'll attach.

00:10:46.946 --> 00:10:48.046
How does that insertion work?

00:10:48.046 --> 00:10:49.996
Or was that part easy,
did people already know

00:10:49.996 --> 00:10:51.346
how to do that?

00:10:51.346 --> 00:10:52.926
- No, not that part is not easy.

00:10:52.926 --> 00:10:55.316
And in fact that's a great question, Sal

00:10:55.316 --> 00:10:58.384
'cause it really gets to the
heart of this technology.

00:10:58.384 --> 00:11:02.596
Because importantly, CRISPR is a cutter,

00:11:02.596 --> 00:11:04.796
it's a cutting technology.

00:11:04.796 --> 00:11:07.846
And the actual genome
editing happens in the cell

00:11:07.846 --> 00:11:10.346
when the cell uses its repair proteins

00:11:10.346 --> 00:11:11.896
to fix the broken DNA.

00:11:11.896 --> 00:11:15.236
So in that example that
we saw that illustrates

00:11:15.236 --> 00:11:19.456
how in a cell, a broken piece
of DNA could be repaired

00:11:19.456 --> 00:11:22.966
by inserting a new section of DNA.

00:11:22.966 --> 00:11:26.206
And there are whole sets
of proteins and cells

00:11:26.206 --> 00:11:29.336
that are responsible for
that kind of chemistry.

00:11:29.336 --> 00:11:31.836
And in truth, we don't fully understand

00:11:31.836 --> 00:11:34.836
how to control that reaction in the cells

00:11:34.836 --> 00:11:37.466
but we know that cells are very good

00:11:37.466 --> 00:11:39.796
at detecting broken DNA and fixing it.

00:11:39.796 --> 00:11:43.686
And there are different
ways to kind of try to

00:11:43.686 --> 00:11:47.526
encourage cells to do the DNA
repair in a particular way

00:11:47.526 --> 00:11:51.766
such as inserting a section
of DNA to make that kind of

00:11:51.766 --> 00:11:53.499
a desired genome edit.

00:11:54.646 --> 00:11:56.586
- No, the thing we
always like to emphasize

00:11:56.586 --> 00:11:58.476
whenever I make a video
on chemistry or biology

00:11:58.476 --> 00:12:00.323
at Khan Academy, in textbooks,

00:12:00.323 --> 00:12:03.892
they kind of make you
believe that these proteins

00:12:03.892 --> 00:12:06.516
have brains of their own
and they know exactly

00:12:06.516 --> 00:12:09.686
where to zero in on but these are these

00:12:09.686 --> 00:12:12.146
thermodynamic chemical
systems where things are

00:12:12.146 --> 00:12:14.566
just constantly vibrating and
colliding with each other.

00:12:14.566 --> 00:12:16.358
And some of those collisions stick

00:12:16.358 --> 00:12:19.126
if the right kind of
things can get close enough

00:12:19.126 --> 00:12:21.926
with not too much energy
to the right things

00:12:21.926 --> 00:12:23.543
and enough energy too sometimes.

00:12:23.543 --> 00:12:26.806
And so it's all of this
random stuff happening.

00:12:26.806 --> 00:12:30.236
And so, yeah, definitely I can
conceptualize how the enzyme

00:12:30.236 --> 00:12:32.586
and the RNA they're part of this complex.

00:12:32.586 --> 00:12:34.416
At some point they're gonna run into

00:12:34.416 --> 00:12:36.506
the right part of the DNA they cut

00:12:36.506 --> 00:12:40.526
and then how do we get that
right DNA sequence in there?

00:12:40.526 --> 00:12:43.146
And it sounds like it is
a little bit stochastic.

00:12:43.146 --> 00:12:46.166
You could have some of
the DNA might repair,

00:12:46.166 --> 00:12:48.776
but if you are able to kind
of have those DNA fragments

00:12:48.776 --> 00:12:51.026
around and the presence
of the right enzymes

00:12:51.026 --> 00:12:52.976
some of that will get stuck in as well.

00:12:54.106 --> 00:12:55.386
- Yes, exactly.

00:12:55.386 --> 00:12:58.626
And so, because of that
scientists are trying to

00:12:58.626 --> 00:13:02.056
manipulate the conditions of
these gene editing experiments

00:13:02.056 --> 00:13:04.096
so that we get the desired outcome.

00:13:04.096 --> 00:13:07.056
Whether it's insertion
of a new section of DNA

00:13:07.056 --> 00:13:10.656
or disruption of a small sequence

00:13:10.656 --> 00:13:12.249
such as a single base pair.

00:13:13.496 --> 00:13:15.616
And there's already a lot
of questions coming in.

00:13:15.616 --> 00:13:17.606
I think a lot of people
are able to extrapolate

00:13:17.606 --> 00:13:20.666
what this could do for
better or for worse maybe

00:13:20.666 --> 00:13:22.006
in certain circumstances.

00:13:22.006 --> 00:13:24.866
So from Facebook,
Michelle Furry is asking,

00:13:24.866 --> 00:13:27.266
can this be done to adults?

00:13:27.266 --> 00:13:30.546
Can the DNA editing be done
to adults or only to embryos?

00:13:30.546 --> 00:13:32.866
Which it goes to this, can
it be done for somatic cells

00:13:32.866 --> 00:13:35.642
which we have trillions of
or do you have to kind of

00:13:35.642 --> 00:13:38.256
get something at the embryonic stage?

00:13:38.256 --> 00:13:39.436
- Great question, Michelle.

00:13:39.436 --> 00:13:43.834
So, the answer is that the
vast majority of genome editing

00:13:43.834 --> 00:13:47.536
for people is going to be, in my opinion,

00:13:47.536 --> 00:13:49.226
certainly for the foreseeable future

00:13:49.226 --> 00:13:53.326
will be in adults or kids,
but certainly not in embryos.

00:13:53.326 --> 00:13:56.246
And so you might wonder,
well, how does that work?

00:13:56.246 --> 00:13:59.946
And typically it would be a treatment

00:13:59.946 --> 00:14:04.946
where cells that can regenerate
or even create a new tissue

00:14:05.766 --> 00:14:07.566
would be the ones being edited.

00:14:07.566 --> 00:14:09.436
So I'll give you an example.

00:14:09.436 --> 00:14:13.556
Sickle cell disease is a
well-known genetic disorder

00:14:13.556 --> 00:14:17.296
of the blood, that results from
a single mutation in a gene

00:14:17.296 --> 00:14:19.846
that encodes one of
this essential proteins

00:14:19.846 --> 00:14:22.537
for carrying oxygen in
our red blood cells.

00:14:22.537 --> 00:14:26.316
And so with sickle cell
disease, CRISPR can be used

00:14:26.316 --> 00:14:30.266
to make correcting mutations
just in those cells

00:14:30.266 --> 00:14:32.796
that give rise to new red blood cells.

00:14:32.796 --> 00:14:37.001
And so that's a way that
therapy can be used in patients

00:14:37.001 --> 00:14:41.089
in a way where it impacts the
patient, but not anybody else.

00:14:42.747 --> 00:14:43.606
- And how is that done?

00:14:43.606 --> 00:14:46.296
Is that done by introducing the enzyme

00:14:46.296 --> 00:14:48.326
directly into the cell somehow?

00:14:48.326 --> 00:14:51.606
Or does it have like a
kind of a harmless virus

00:14:51.606 --> 00:14:52.646
that can kind of take it in?

00:14:52.646 --> 00:14:56.386
How do you introduce it
into the somatic cells,

00:14:56.386 --> 00:14:59.146
the body cells, these
potentially trillions of cells

00:14:59.146 --> 00:15:00.766
that might be involved here?

00:15:00.766 --> 00:15:03.066
- Yeah, well right now
the way it's being done

00:15:03.066 --> 00:15:06.216
is by taking those blood precursor cells

00:15:06.216 --> 00:15:09.236
we call them blood stem
cells out of a patient

00:15:09.236 --> 00:15:11.909
and doing the genome
editing in the laboratory.

00:15:13.688 --> 00:15:17.197
The CRISPR protein and it's
guiding RNA can be introduced

00:15:17.197 --> 00:15:18.586
in different ways.

00:15:18.586 --> 00:15:22.676
It can be put in as a directly
assembled molecular complex

00:15:22.676 --> 00:15:24.006
of the protein and the RNA.

00:15:24.006 --> 00:15:26.076
Or it can be put in, as you mentioned Sal,

00:15:26.076 --> 00:15:29.009
using what we call a vector, for example

00:15:29.009 --> 00:15:31.969
a virus that can encode those materials.

00:15:33.056 --> 00:15:34.552
- So y'all will take the stem cells out.

00:15:34.552 --> 00:15:36.646
And I mean, you're able to do it like

00:15:36.646 --> 00:15:37.986
you're not doing one cell at a time

00:15:37.986 --> 00:15:40.860
you're able to do like
globs of it, I guess.

00:15:40.860 --> 00:15:42.827
I think that's a technical
term, right, globs?

00:15:42.827 --> 00:15:45.079
- Well, it sounds kind of technical.

00:15:48.119 --> 00:15:50.509
- By either with the
virus or introducing it.

00:15:51.916 --> 00:15:53.596
I am curious because I know

00:15:55.145 --> 00:15:57.156
I've had you could tell
people, I hang out with,

00:15:57.156 --> 00:15:59.388
I've had debates with my
friends overnight about

00:15:59.388 --> 00:16:02.016
when you do it the way you just described

00:16:02.016 --> 00:16:03.696
it affects that one individual.

00:16:03.696 --> 00:16:05.566
And hopefully it cures them of sickle cell

00:16:05.566 --> 00:16:08.516
which could be a very bad
disease, so that's huge.

00:16:08.516 --> 00:16:10.026
There's the other possibility

00:16:10.026 --> 00:16:11.426
of you're going to the germline.

00:16:11.426 --> 00:16:13.156
You're either going directly to an embryo

00:16:13.156 --> 00:16:16.456
or you're going to the cells that produce

00:16:16.456 --> 00:16:18.536
the sperms and the eggs.

00:16:18.536 --> 00:16:22.536
And then that in theory,
you're effecting the gene pool.

00:16:22.536 --> 00:16:27.536
And so one question is, is that doable?

00:16:27.586 --> 00:16:28.796
And then what are your thoughts on that?

00:16:28.796 --> 00:16:30.846
Because there's a possibility
of removing sickle cell

00:16:30.846 --> 00:16:32.479
from the gene pool entirely.

00:16:33.783 --> 00:16:36.083
But where do you see the
puts and takes there?

00:16:37.371 --> 00:16:41.239
- What you just described
is what we call germline

00:16:41.239 --> 00:16:43.036
or germ cell editing.

00:16:43.036 --> 00:16:44.566
And we call them germ cells

00:16:44.566 --> 00:16:47.396
because they're literally
able to give rise

00:16:47.396 --> 00:16:50.826
to many more cells that
are of a particular type.

00:16:50.826 --> 00:16:55.826
You can create a whole organism,
like a whole human being.

00:16:56.923 --> 00:17:00.296
And the reason that that is in my view

00:17:00.296 --> 00:17:04.036
a much more profound use of CRISPR is that

00:17:04.036 --> 00:17:07.216
it creates what we call a
heritable change in the DNA.

00:17:07.216 --> 00:17:09.316
So it affects not just the individual,

00:17:09.316 --> 00:17:12.600
but all of their kids
and their kids' kids.

00:17:12.600 --> 00:17:16.476
Now, if you could remove a harmful gene

00:17:16.476 --> 00:17:19.176
from the human population

00:17:19.176 --> 00:17:21.296
and that would obviously take time

00:17:21.296 --> 00:17:25.678
but you could in principle
do this using genome editing.

00:17:25.678 --> 00:17:29.426
It could be argued that
there might be cases

00:17:29.426 --> 00:17:31.326
where that would be desirable.

00:17:31.326 --> 00:17:34.296
But I think right now, for many reasons

00:17:34.296 --> 00:17:35.776
some of them are technical.

00:17:35.776 --> 00:17:38.718
And of course there are
many ethical implications.

00:17:38.718 --> 00:17:41.426
I think most scientists
feel that that is not

00:17:41.426 --> 00:17:44.516
an appropriate use of
CRISPR in humans right now.

00:17:44.516 --> 00:17:46.966
And that we should focus
on somatic cell editing.

00:17:48.006 --> 00:17:49.501
- And this is already leading to,

00:17:49.501 --> 00:17:51.506
I remember being at a conference

00:17:51.506 --> 00:17:52.466
and someone was talking about this

00:17:52.466 --> 00:17:55.636
there are already people
being treated for sickle cell

00:17:55.636 --> 00:17:56.825
because of this.

00:17:56.825 --> 00:17:58.396
- Right, it's extraordinary.

00:17:58.396 --> 00:18:00.646
It's very exciting for all
of us working in the field

00:18:00.646 --> 00:18:03.826
to see this work already
benefiting patients.

00:18:03.826 --> 00:18:05.886
And we know there will
be many more to benefit

00:18:05.886 --> 00:18:06.719
in the future.

00:18:08.026 --> 00:18:10.158
- Absolutely, and on the ethics of it,

00:18:10.158 --> 00:18:11.056
you have this question from Facebook,

00:18:11.056 --> 00:18:14.366
Jeffrey Kurtzberg is asking
who should decide the ethics

00:18:14.366 --> 00:18:17.286
of the science, just
because we can, should we

00:18:17.286 --> 00:18:21.176
the butterfly effect may not
be a strong enough analogy.

00:18:21.176 --> 00:18:23.556
And I think what he's referring
to is you can edit one gene.

00:18:23.556 --> 00:18:26.156
It seems to do one thing, but
these are complex systems.

00:18:26.156 --> 00:18:28.896
And obviously there was that,
I don't know if you folks know

00:18:28.896 --> 00:18:31.808
there was that case in
China where a scientist

00:18:31.808 --> 00:18:35.969
kind of went rogue used this
technology to edit an embryo

00:18:35.969 --> 00:18:40.246
to have a more resistance towards AIDS.

00:18:40.246 --> 00:18:42.676
And actually I read that
scientist was put in prison

00:18:42.676 --> 00:18:45.086
because of the questionable
ethics of this.

00:18:45.086 --> 00:18:48.154
So one, it seems like
the cat's out of the bag,

00:18:48.154 --> 00:18:51.156
this is something that can be
done by scientists anywhere

00:18:51.156 --> 00:18:54.479
you don't need billions of
dollars of capital to do CRISPR.

00:18:55.736 --> 00:18:57.236
Who do you think should decide it?

00:18:57.236 --> 00:18:59.146
What do you think is going to happen is

00:18:59.146 --> 00:19:02.597
if there's countries, jurisdictions
where people don't care.

00:19:02.597 --> 00:19:05.449
How can we regulate this type of thing?

00:19:06.936 --> 00:19:08.866
- I think so, first of all,

00:19:08.866 --> 00:19:10.036
thanks for the question, Jeffrey.

00:19:10.036 --> 00:19:12.806
I think the situation with CRISPR

00:19:12.806 --> 00:19:16.446
is in a way analogous to what we see

00:19:16.446 --> 00:19:18.456
for a number of types of technologies

00:19:18.456 --> 00:19:20.294
whether they're in the biological sciences

00:19:20.294 --> 00:19:24.866
or in technology computing
with for example,

00:19:24.866 --> 00:19:27.396
artificial intelligence
where the question is

00:19:28.286 --> 00:19:29.646
the technology is powerful.

00:19:29.646 --> 00:19:31.886
There are many opportunities to use it

00:19:31.886 --> 00:19:35.206
to do things that will
be beneficial to humanity

00:19:35.206 --> 00:19:36.536
but there also are risks.

00:19:36.536 --> 00:19:38.526
And how do we control those risks

00:19:38.526 --> 00:19:41.076
and who decides about uses?

00:19:41.076 --> 00:19:42.956
And I guess for me with CRISPR,

00:19:42.956 --> 00:19:46.026
I feel strongly that it has to,

00:19:46.026 --> 00:19:49.176
that those decisions
really do need to be made

00:19:50.745 --> 00:19:52.676
by all the stakeholders that are involved.

00:19:52.676 --> 00:19:55.096
So in the end, that
kind of means all of us

00:19:55.096 --> 00:19:58.896
and that will require scientists to engage

00:19:58.896 --> 00:20:01.016
and discuss the science itself

00:20:01.016 --> 00:20:04.418
and kind of the way the technology works.

00:20:04.418 --> 00:20:07.466
And also to work closely with regulators

00:20:07.466 --> 00:20:12.466
and all sorts of groups that
are thinking about in this case

00:20:13.266 --> 00:20:17.866
how genome editing could
impact people in the future

00:20:17.866 --> 00:20:19.781
so that we can make responsible decisions.

00:20:19.781 --> 00:20:22.856
And that sounds hard and it is.

00:20:22.856 --> 00:20:26.216
But I think a way to start is by engaging

00:20:26.216 --> 00:20:28.766
the scientific community globally

00:20:28.766 --> 00:20:30.896
to think about this and
work on it together.

00:20:30.896 --> 00:20:33.696
And I've been actually very
pleased over the last few years

00:20:33.696 --> 00:20:37.056
that in discussions that I started

00:20:37.056 --> 00:20:40.886
with my colleagues back
in, I think it was 2015

00:20:40.886 --> 00:20:42.236
we've been able to engage

00:20:42.236 --> 00:20:45.696
with now the World Health
Organization, with UNESCO

00:20:45.696 --> 00:20:49.116
and with many of the scientific
societies around the world

00:20:49.116 --> 00:20:50.676
to come together.

00:20:50.676 --> 00:20:52.556
There've been many conferences

00:20:52.556 --> 00:20:55.926
and committees that have been
convened to look into this.

00:20:55.926 --> 00:20:58.696
And we now have, I think,
a very good framework

00:20:58.696 --> 00:21:02.029
for using CRISPR in different
settings going forward.

00:21:03.796 --> 00:21:05.566
- And there's tons of
questions are coming in

00:21:05.566 --> 00:21:07.696
'cause this is such a fascinating topic.

00:21:07.696 --> 00:21:10.712
I'm curious and there's several
questions along this lines.

00:21:10.712 --> 00:21:14.106
Well, one beyond sickle cell

00:21:14.106 --> 00:21:15.866
where do you think is the next obvious?

00:21:15.866 --> 00:21:18.206
Sickle cell seems, seems
it is very compelling

00:21:18.206 --> 00:21:20.166
'cause it's one mutation,
as you mentioned.

00:21:20.166 --> 00:21:22.326
It seems to have a clear negative

00:21:22.326 --> 00:21:24.706
if you can fix it, clear positive.

00:21:24.706 --> 00:21:26.716
What are the next things
that you think CRISPR

00:21:26.716 --> 00:21:27.616
will be able to influence?

00:21:27.616 --> 00:21:30.286
And I am also curious, I think
everyone watching is curious

00:21:30.286 --> 00:21:32.246
what's the next frontier for you?

00:21:32.246 --> 00:21:33.346
Where do you think this can go next?

00:21:33.346 --> 00:21:34.746
What's your research now on?

00:21:36.086 --> 00:21:38.838
- Well, I'll start with
the first question.

00:21:38.838 --> 00:21:43.838
There are a few thousand human
diseases that are well known

00:21:44.526 --> 00:21:47.396
to be caused by a single genetic mutation

00:21:47.396 --> 00:21:50.936
a single gene whose correction could lead

00:21:50.936 --> 00:21:52.186
to a therapeutic benefit.

00:21:52.186 --> 00:21:56.679
So those are I think obvious
initial targets for CRISPR.

00:21:57.836 --> 00:22:00.596
The big challenge is the
challenge of delivery

00:22:00.596 --> 00:22:03.286
which means how do we
get the CRISPR molecules

00:22:03.286 --> 00:22:05.746
into the cells that need editing?

00:22:05.746 --> 00:22:07.936
So the example of sickle
cell is a good one

00:22:07.936 --> 00:22:12.627
because it provides a
way to do the editing

00:22:12.627 --> 00:22:15.413
outside of the patient because of

00:22:15.413 --> 00:22:18.036
the need to edit the cells of the blood

00:22:18.036 --> 00:22:21.586
which can be easily found and
then taken into the laboratory

00:22:21.586 --> 00:22:23.996
and edited and then replaced.

00:22:23.996 --> 00:22:28.156
But what if we want to
edit a gene in brain cells

00:22:28.156 --> 00:22:31.146
or in heart cells or muscle cells

00:22:31.146 --> 00:22:34.146
it's a lot harder to
do that in the patient.

00:22:34.146 --> 00:22:38.366
So that's, I think in many
ways where the bottleneck is

00:22:38.366 --> 00:22:40.896
right now in the genome editing field.

00:22:40.896 --> 00:22:45.826
So, in terms of the next maybe
near term targets for CRISPR,

00:22:45.826 --> 00:22:49.456
I certainly think other blood
disorders will be addressed

00:22:49.456 --> 00:22:51.726
as well as diseases of the eye

00:22:51.726 --> 00:22:54.186
because this is again a
tissue where potentially

00:22:54.186 --> 00:22:57.983
one can deliver the CRISPR
molecules locally by injection.

00:22:57.983 --> 00:23:00.756
And maybe in the not too distant future

00:23:00.756 --> 00:23:02.366
maybe a few years from now,

00:23:02.366 --> 00:23:05.606
we'll see therapies for
muscular dystrophy coming along.

00:23:05.606 --> 00:23:07.916
There are a number of groups and companies

00:23:07.916 --> 00:23:10.946
that are working very
actively on muscular dystrophy

00:23:10.946 --> 00:23:15.946
because of the potential
to deliver CRISPR molecules

00:23:15.966 --> 00:23:17.486
into muscle cells.

00:23:17.486 --> 00:23:19.296
And doing that using viruses

00:23:19.296 --> 00:23:22.002
that specifically go into muscle cells.

00:23:22.002 --> 00:23:23.896
And in terms of my own research,

00:23:23.896 --> 00:23:27.376
I'm continuing to work on
the fundamentals of CRISPR

00:23:27.376 --> 00:23:28.936
and understanding how it works.

00:23:28.936 --> 00:23:31.197
But we're also engaging increasingly

00:23:31.197 --> 00:23:33.356
in this challenge of delivery.

00:23:33.356 --> 00:23:36.176
Because I think that for me as a scientist

00:23:36.176 --> 00:23:39.656
I really wanna see this
technology be affordable

00:23:39.656 --> 00:23:42.563
and available to everybody
around the world that needs it.

00:23:42.563 --> 00:23:45.246
That's a tall order, but I think one step

00:23:45.246 --> 00:23:48.706
towards doing that is making
sure that we can deliver it

00:23:48.706 --> 00:23:51.789
into a cell type where
it can be beneficial.

00:23:53.201 --> 00:23:55.126
- It's important to
note, we've talked a lot

00:23:55.126 --> 00:23:57.476
about the implications for
CRISPR to kind of change

00:23:57.476 --> 00:24:00.166
and maybe, quote improve,
but maybe sometimes

00:24:01.046 --> 00:24:02.446
people can go rogue.

00:24:02.446 --> 00:24:06.086
It also allows us to
understand one of main things

00:24:06.086 --> 00:24:08.026
where we're so afraid
to edit gene genetics

00:24:08.026 --> 00:24:10.066
is because it's such a complex system,

00:24:10.066 --> 00:24:11.836
but obviously outside of humans,

00:24:11.836 --> 00:24:14.726
you could do it in other
models and other animals

00:24:14.726 --> 00:24:16.526
or in bacteria.

00:24:16.526 --> 00:24:20.616
You can now edit and kind
of run, kind of AB testing

00:24:20.616 --> 00:24:21.876
the way that you do in software.

00:24:21.876 --> 00:24:23.016
Like, what if I change this?

00:24:23.016 --> 00:24:24.533
How does that happen?

00:24:24.533 --> 00:24:25.605
What if I change this?

00:24:25.605 --> 00:24:26.796
What if I change this and this is?

00:24:26.796 --> 00:24:28.986
Are you seeing that type of
research accelerate dramatically

00:24:28.986 --> 00:24:30.706
because of CRISPR?

00:24:30.706 --> 00:24:34.126
- Hugely, hugely, I mean,
this is something that

00:24:34.126 --> 00:24:35.956
over the last eight years we've just seen

00:24:35.956 --> 00:24:39.356
research taking off at
an extraordinary pace,

00:24:39.356 --> 00:24:41.576
doing exactly what you just said Sal.

00:24:41.576 --> 00:24:44.456
So because now that we
have this technology

00:24:44.456 --> 00:24:48.134
for precise manipulation
of one or even many genes

00:24:48.134 --> 00:24:51.946
in cells in the laboratory,
it's an incredible tool.

00:24:51.946 --> 00:24:54.766
It means that we can now manipulate genes

00:24:54.766 --> 00:24:57.157
and understand gene functions

00:24:57.157 --> 00:25:00.336
in ways that previously would
have been very difficult

00:25:00.336 --> 00:25:03.596
or in some cases impossible
really to figure out.

00:25:05.389 --> 00:25:07.666
- In the time we have, we
have a lot of young people

00:25:07.666 --> 00:25:09.446
watching and a lot of
parents of young people

00:25:09.446 --> 00:25:11.954
have this question from
Facebook, Carol Smith Kapur,

00:25:11.954 --> 00:25:16.954
I'll paraphrase, they have a
son who wants to do what...

00:25:17.696 --> 00:25:19.396
Actually I just lost the question.

00:25:20.676 --> 00:25:22.156
Well, I'll paraphrase.

00:25:22.156 --> 00:25:24.916
Who wants to go into this type of a field?

00:25:24.916 --> 00:25:25.986
What would you say to a young person

00:25:25.986 --> 00:25:27.546
who's fascinated by this?

00:25:27.546 --> 00:25:28.766
I've heard people say

00:25:28.766 --> 00:25:31.476
what computing has been the
last two or three decades

00:25:31.476 --> 00:25:32.596
the next two or three decades

00:25:32.596 --> 00:25:34.546
is going to be all about
genetic technology.

00:25:34.546 --> 00:25:36.196
What do you recommend to
someone in high school?

00:25:36.196 --> 00:25:37.286
What should they focus on?

00:25:37.286 --> 00:25:38.119
What should they study?

00:25:38.119 --> 00:25:39.776
What are good majors to be in?

00:25:39.776 --> 00:25:41.911
And what do you think
the careers will be like

00:25:41.911 --> 00:25:44.279
as you go in the decades ahead?

00:25:45.456 --> 00:25:46.779
- Thanks for the question, Carol.

00:25:46.779 --> 00:25:49.696
there's so many
opportunities for young folks

00:25:49.696 --> 00:25:51.676
who are getting into science right now

00:25:51.676 --> 00:25:53.696
or thinking about that.

00:25:53.696 --> 00:25:55.559
Is science the right career for me?

00:25:55.559 --> 00:25:58.396
And like you said, Sal, I think,

00:25:58.396 --> 00:26:01.356
it's just I think many of us
that are working in this field

00:26:01.356 --> 00:26:04.846
feel that there are just
exploding opportunities.

00:26:04.846 --> 00:26:08.476
There's many things that
were difficult to do

00:26:08.476 --> 00:26:11.466
even a year or two ago
that we're now able to do

00:26:11.466 --> 00:26:14.516
as scientists in terms of our research.

00:26:14.516 --> 00:26:17.496
So for students, I would
say, pursue your...

00:26:17.496 --> 00:26:20.476
I always tell my own students, my own son,

00:26:20.476 --> 00:26:21.608
pursue your passions.

00:26:21.608 --> 00:26:26.582
And if it's biology that you
get really excited about,

00:26:26.582 --> 00:26:30.385
pursue that, if it's chemistry,
for me it was chemistry

00:26:30.385 --> 00:26:31.856
in those early days.

00:26:31.856 --> 00:26:34.495
And wanting to understand
how molecules work.

00:26:34.495 --> 00:26:37.726
And I've always found for myself that

00:26:37.726 --> 00:26:42.726
by really following my own kind
of gut feelings about things

00:26:42.916 --> 00:26:46.486
what I felt really passionately
excited about studying,

00:26:46.486 --> 00:26:49.936
that has always led me in
interesting directions.

00:26:49.936 --> 00:26:52.376
Not always directions that I
could have predicted either

00:26:52.376 --> 00:26:54.889
which kind of makes science fun.

00:26:56.166 --> 00:26:58.666
- Absolutely, and maybe
in my last question

00:26:58.666 --> 00:27:02.146
I am curious, how does it feel?

00:27:02.146 --> 00:27:04.196
Do you ever sit there
and look in the mirror

00:27:04.196 --> 00:27:06.546
and say, yeah, I just
changed humanity forever.

00:27:10.866 --> 00:27:12.339
- Well, I have to say that

00:27:12.339 --> 00:27:16.296
when we were first doing this
research in the laboratory

00:27:16.296 --> 00:27:18.236
there were a number of moments for me

00:27:18.236 --> 00:27:20.740
where I was kind of
pinching myself and saying,

00:27:20.740 --> 00:27:23.540
is this really happening?

00:27:23.540 --> 00:27:27.136
It seemed kind of fantastical
to think that bacteria

00:27:27.136 --> 00:27:30.915
had evolved a system for detecting viruses

00:27:30.915 --> 00:27:34.046
that we could then
figure out how it worked

00:27:34.046 --> 00:27:35.246
in the laboratory.

00:27:35.246 --> 00:27:38.116
And that that insight would
then allow us to harness it

00:27:38.116 --> 00:27:39.716
as a powerful technology.

00:27:39.716 --> 00:27:43.336
So, it was really an incredible experience

00:27:43.336 --> 00:27:44.686
to be involved in that.

00:27:44.686 --> 00:27:47.846
And yeah, again to me, I would just,

00:27:47.846 --> 00:27:50.656
again getting back to why I'm a scientist

00:27:50.656 --> 00:27:52.836
and why I think it's
such a wonderful career

00:27:52.836 --> 00:27:56.113
for students that are thinking about this,

00:27:56.113 --> 00:27:58.686
it's a way to continue to have

00:27:58.686 --> 00:28:03.116
the kind of curiosity driven
passions that you have

00:28:03.116 --> 00:28:05.276
when you're growing up and you get to

00:28:05.276 --> 00:28:07.750
as a scientist people still pay me to

00:28:07.750 --> 00:28:11.429
ask questions and try to
figure out how things work.

00:28:11.429 --> 00:28:14.343
It's really a fun career.

00:28:15.696 --> 00:28:17.816
- Well, you can feel the energy.

00:28:17.816 --> 00:28:19.866
And I mean, I think it makes sense

00:28:19.866 --> 00:28:22.774
'cause you are literally
changing the world.

00:28:22.774 --> 00:28:25.406
And there are people
speaking hyperbolic terms

00:28:25.406 --> 00:28:27.926
about a lot of things,
but what you have done,

00:28:27.926 --> 00:28:30.616
I do think in 100 or 200 years,
a lot of what we're doing

00:28:30.616 --> 00:28:33.096
in our current time might be forgotten,

00:28:33.096 --> 00:28:35.496
but this is gonna be one of
these things that will change

00:28:35.496 --> 00:28:38.726
hopefully and I believe
forever, positively humanity.

00:28:38.726 --> 00:28:40.546
So thank you so much for your work

00:28:40.546 --> 00:28:43.316
and thank you for joining
us and inspiring us.

00:28:43.316 --> 00:28:45.816
- Thanks for inviting me
so Sal, great to be here.

00:28:48.516 --> 00:28:50.656
- So thanks everyone for joining.

00:28:50.656 --> 00:28:53.401
If you couldn't tell, I was
a little bit star struck

00:28:53.401 --> 00:28:56.516
for anyone who's followed
what's been going on

00:28:56.516 --> 00:28:59.196
in biology and chemistry
over the last several years.

00:28:59.196 --> 00:29:01.196
What Jennifer's work with her colleagues

00:29:02.406 --> 00:29:04.046
it's gonna be a big deal

00:29:04.046 --> 00:29:05.696
and you're only gonna hear
more and more about it.

00:29:05.696 --> 00:29:07.956
So look, I encourage you

00:29:07.956 --> 00:29:10.296
if any of what we said is confusing

00:29:10.296 --> 00:29:12.396
definitely you can review

00:29:12.396 --> 00:29:15.343
the foundations on Khan
Academy on your chemistry

00:29:15.343 --> 00:29:17.536
and the biology on Khan Academy.

00:29:17.536 --> 00:29:18.936
But I encourage you to look into that

00:29:18.936 --> 00:29:20.906
because I have a feeling in many fields

00:29:20.906 --> 00:29:23.126
especially if you're going
into biotech or medicine

00:29:23.126 --> 00:29:24.746
of the war Jennifer's work and CRISPR

00:29:24.746 --> 00:29:25.916
is going to be a central element.

00:29:25.916 --> 00:29:28.411
So it's a good thing to get smart on.

00:29:28.411 --> 00:29:30.646
So with that, thanks so much for joining.

00:29:30.646 --> 00:29:34.596
And I will see all of
y'all I believe next week.

00:29:34.596 --> 00:29:38.196
I live day to day, I don't
know what I'm doing tomorrow.

00:29:38.196 --> 00:29:40.879
But I will see you all, have a good day.

